<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Nullroute — the DAC half of the /data/misc/nullroute access story.
    Installs to /system_ext/etc/permissions/nullroute-gid.xml

    THE PROBLEM
    ===========
    The four state dirs /data/misc/nullroute/{index,ctl,log,priv} are 0770
    system:misc. Nothing that is not uid system, uid root or a member of gid misc
    (AID_MISC = 9998) can open, list or create anything inside them.

    (Be precise about the parent, because the mode is easy to misread: /data/misc
    is 01771 system:misc — owner rwx, group rwx, OTHER x-only — so any process can
    *traverse* /data/misc and /data/misc/nullroute. Traversal is not the gate. The
    0770 on the four leaf directories is. Do not "fix" the parent's mode expecting
    it to change anything, and do not relax the leaves expecting the parent to
    still protect them.)

    SELinux is only half the gate. A process can hold
    `allow nullroute_app nullroute_ctl_file:file { ... map ... }` and still get
    EACCES from ordinary Unix permissions. Both halves have to line up.

    THE REJECTED FIX
    ================
    android:sharedUserId="android.uid.system" would give the app uid 1000 and
    solve this in one line. It is refused:
      * deprecated and permanently un-migratable once shipped;
      * it collapses the app into the system UID's sandbox, so a bug in the
        blocklist parser — which chews on hundreds of thousands of lines of
        attacker-influenced text from the internet — becomes a system-UID
        compromise;
      * it interferes with the /data/app sideload-update path we rely on for
        out-of-band APK updates.
    The sibling in-tree app packages/apps/Freezer correctly does not use it.

    THE FIX
    =======
    SystemConfig parses <permission> elements with <group gid="..."/> children
    from every etc/permissions directory it reads with the ALLOW_PERMISSIONS
    flag, and /system_ext/etc/permissions is read with that flag. PackageManager
    then adds the mapped gid to the supplementary group list of any package
    holding the permission. This is the same machinery that maps
    android.permission.INTERNET -> gid inet; we are not inventing a mechanism.

    The app must ALSO declare the permission itself, with
    android:protectionLevel="signature", and <uses-permission> it. The signature
    check is trivially satisfied — the app is its own signer — and declaring it
    in the manifest means the permission exists even if this file is somehow not
    read, which turns a silent gid failure into a loud missing-permission one.

    VERIFY ON DEVICE (Spike 2, SPEC.md §10.6):
        adb shell cat /proc/$(adb shell pidof com.bestrom.nullroute)/status | grep Groups
    must list 9998.

    FALLBACK IF THE MAPPING DOES NOT TAKE
    =====================================
    Relax the four state dirs from 0770 to 0775 in rom/init.nullroute.rc and let
    SELinux be the sole gate. Note what that actually costs: /data/misc is
    world-traversable (01771 grants "other" execute), so the leaf mode is the only
    DAC left and 0775 removes it entirely. It is safe on a user build because MAC is
    enforcing and no app domain is granted these types — but it is a fallback,
    not the design, and it is strictly one layer thinner.

    THE SECOND ENTRY — the nrctl control channel
    ============================================
    `nrctl pause|resume|update` does not write anything itself; it broadcasts
    com.bestrom.nullroute.action.CTL to the app's CtlReceiver, which is guarded by
    android:permission="com.bestrom.nullroute.permission.CTL".

    ActivityManager.checkComponentPermission() short-circuits to GRANTED for
    appId 0 (root) and appId 1000 (system) — so `su` and system_server pass that
    guard for free. **uid 2000 (shell) does not.** com.android.shell cannot
    <uses-permission> a permission that did not exist when it was built, so
    without the assignment below the broadcast is simply not delivered: `am`
    prints "Broadcast completed: result=0", nrctl reports success, and nothing
    happens. A mutating CLI that silently no-ops is exactly the v8.2 failure this
    project's CLI design is built to make impossible, so the fix belongs here and
    not in a doc comment.

    <assign-permission> is parsed from any etc/permissions directory read with
    ALLOW_PERMISSIONS, which /system_ext/etc/permissions is, and it is applied by
    PermissionManagerService.checkUidPermission() for uids that are not packages.

    VERIFY ON DEVICE:
        adb shell nrctl pause 1 && adb shell nrctl status   # must read PAUSED
-->
<permissions>
    <permission name="com.bestrom.nullroute.permission.STATE">
        <!-- AID_MISC = 9998, the group that owns /data/misc. -->
        <group gid="misc" />
    </permission>

    <assign-permission name="com.bestrom.nullroute.permission.CTL" uid="shell" />
</permissions>
